First, on-device AI processes data locally, so it doesn't need an internet connection. That means things like real-time translation or voice commands can work even without a network. For example, if a user is in a remote area, the phone can still use the AI without relying on the cloud. That's one point.

Next, privacy is a big factor. On-device AI doesn't send data to the cloud, so user data stays on the phone. This is a key advantage. So, for instance, if a phone uses on-device AI for voice recognition, the data isn't transmitted, which is better for privacy.

Then, there's the aspect of latency. On-device processing is faster because it doesn't have to wait for cloud servers. So real-time applications like AR or gaming can be more responsive. That's another point.

